24.2.m:
By the cow for purchasing Soma the third pressing is to be obtained.
Cattle indeed are the third pressing.
Because he purchases with a cow,
by that alone he obtains the third pressing.
He measures [Soma] on a red hide.
Therefore cattle are mostly of red form.
He should not consent to its being picked apart.
He should not be an onlooker of it while being picked apart.
Soma is the sovereign lord of plants.
He tears away what is swallowed by him; he becomes hungry.
Therefore the Soma-seller is hungry.
He purchases near water;
he purchases it possessed of sap.
He purchases with gold;
he purchases it possessed of splendour.
He purchases with a she-goat;
he purchases it possessed of ascetic heat.
He purchases with a milch-cow;
he purchases it possessed of the milk-mixture.
He purchases with a draft-ox;
he obtains the bearer of the sacrifice.
He purchases with a young bull and a young cow;
he purchases a pair for him.
He purchases with a bull;
he purchases it possessed of Indra.
As to that, they say: ‘He bargains with Prajāpati who bargains with a bull.’
Whatever [male] has testicles but is devoid of semen, with that he should purchase;
by that alone he purchases it possessed of Indra,
[and] does not bargain with Prajāpati.
He purchases with ten [articles];
the Virāj has ten syllables;
the Virāj is food;
he becomes established in the Virāj, in food-consumption.

24.3.m:
‘You are swift,’ [he says].
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
for by speech one hastens this way and that.
Looking at the cow for purchasing Soma, he offers an oblation.
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
looking at her, he grasps her.
‘Held by the mind,’ [he says];
for by the mind speech is held.
‘Pleasing to Viṣṇu,’ [he says].
Viṣṇu indeed is the sacrifice;
he makes her pleasing to the sacrifice.
‘In the impulse of you, whose strength is true, may I attain the support of the body, svāhā!’ [he says].
He who attains the support of her body flourishes.
Impelled by Savitṛ, he attains the support of her body.
With the svāhā-call he grasps her.
Having announced it to yonder Āditya, Soma is to be purchased:
‘O divine Sūrya, we shall purchase Soma.’
Having announced it to yonder Āditya, he purchases Soma.
‘You are thought,’ [he says].
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
he instructs her;
therefore instructed offspring are born.
‘You are intellect,’ [he says];
for by the mind speech is held.
‘You are contemplation,’ [he says];
for by speech one contemplates this way and that.
‘You are the sacrificial fee,’ [he says];
for she is the sacrificial fee.
‘You are worthy of sacrifice,’ [he says];
for she is worthy of sacrifice.
‘You belong to the ruling class,’ [he says];
for she belongs to the ruling class.
Moreover, whom she favours, he becomes a noble.
‘You are Aditi, facing both ways,’ [he says];
for she is Aditi, facing both ways;
for on both sides she encompasses the sacrifice;
the introductory [sacrifice] belongs to Aditi, the concluding [sacrifice] belongs to Aditi.
‘Become well-turned to the east, well-turned to the west for me,’ [he says].
He makes her well-turned to the east and well-turned to the west.
‘May Mitra bind you by the foot,’ [he says].
If she were bound by the foot, she would belong to the fathers.
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
the snare belongs to Varuṇa;
speech seized by Varuṇa would not speak.
If she were seized by the ear, she would be Vārtraghnī.
If she were unchecked [and] restrained—
‘May Mitra bind you by the foot,’ [he says].
Mitra indeed is the sacred lore;
by sacred lore alone he binds her by the foot.
‘May Pūṣan protect the paths,’ [he says].
This [earth] indeed is Pūṣan;
to her he entrusts her.
‘For Indra, the overseer,’ [he says].
Indra indeed is the mightiest of the gods;
him he makes her overseer.
‘May your mother consent, may your father [consent],’ [he says];
for both her father and mother are those who give consent.
‘Go, O goddess, to the god, to Soma for Indra,’ [he says];
for as a goddess she goes to the god.
‘To Soma for Indra; may Rudra turn you back,’ [he says].
Rudra indeed is the swiftest of the gods;
him he places beyond her.
[‘For the other shoots,’ he says.]
‘Return safely, accompanied by Soma,’ [he says].
He brings well-being for her return.

26.3.m:
The consecrated one should not offer an oblation.
The consecrated one indeed is an oblation; Rudra is Agni.
If he were to offer, he would surrender himself, become an oblation, to Rudra.
If he were not to offer, he would exclude a joint of the sacrifice.
If the Soma has been purchased, he should offer in the fire of the consecrated one.
For then the sacrifice has been released;
the oblations go forth.
If it has not been purchased, taking the two churning-sticks and clarified butter, going away and churning fire, he should offer at the end of the sacrificial post with a verse to Viṣṇu.
For the sacrificial post belongs to Viṣṇu;
he approaches it with its own deity.
"I have gone past others, I have not approached others," [he says].
For he goes past others and does not approach others.
"Those whom I have not favored, them I have passed by," [he says].
For those whom he does not favor, he passes by.
"Hitherward from the farther ones have I found you, beyond the nearer ones," [he says].
For he finds it hitherward from the farther ones, beyond the nearer ones.
"Thee, such, O tree, we favor for the worship of the gods," [he says].
For he favors it for the worship of the gods.
"Welcome to Viṣṇu," [he says].
Viṣṇu indeed is the sacrifice;
he makes it welcome to the sacrifice itself.
"May the god Savitṛ anoint thee with honey," [he says].
Ghee indeed is the honey of the gods;
he makes it pure and worthy of sacrifice.
"O plant, protect it," [he says].
He makes, as it were, armor [for it].
"O axe, harm it not," [he says].
The axe indeed is a thunderbolt;
[this is] for non-injury.
The chip that falls away first, that he should bring.
For vigor flies away following this chip of it that flies away;
he erects it possessed of vigor.
That which is bent to the south, he should not cut;
it belongs to the pit.
That which is bent to the north, not that;
it belongs to a pillar.
That which has branches pointing upward, chips pointing upward, that he should cut;
that indeed is fit for a sacrificial post;
he cuts that which is fit for a sacrificial post.
That which has an undried top, many branches, and many leaves, that he should cut;
plants indeed are trees, cattle are plants;
he enriches him with cattle.
That which has a dried top, no branches, and no leaves, that he should cut of him whom he hates;
trees are plants, cattle are plants;
he deprives him of cattle.
That which is bent toward the north-east, that he should cut;
that indeed is inclined toward the sacrificial essence;
he cuts that which is inclined toward the sacrificial essence.
"Graze not the sky, harm not the atmosphere, unite with the earth," [he says].
The sacrificial post indeed is a thunderbolt;
[this is] for the non-injury of these worlds.
He props it up toward the north, toward the east;
that indeed is the world of the gods;
he props it up right toward the world of the gods.
"O tree, grow up with a hundred shoots," [he says, and] offers into the stump;
he places abundance right in the trees;
therefore more grow from this stump.
"May we grow up with a thousand shoots," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
"Whom this axe, sharpened, has led forward to great fortune," [he says].
For he is led forward to great fortune who is led forward to the sacrifice.
One that is a parasite, that he should cut of him whom he hates;
homeless indeed and seatless is that which is a parasite;
he makes him homeless and seatless.
That which has grown by itself, that he should cut;
that indeed has a home and a seat which has grown by itself;
he makes the sacrificer possessed of a home and possessed of a seat.
He should cut it at ankle-height;
for the ankle is a joint;
[it is then] measured by a joint of the sacrifice.
He should cut it at knee-height;
for the knee is a joint;
[it is then] measured by a joint of the sacrifice.
He should cut it at as much as where an axle would touch;
[it is then] measured by a joint of the sacrifice,
for the non-repulsion of cattle.

26.4.m:
It should be cut commensurate with cattle;
[namely] of ten cubits.
Ten indeed are the vital breaths of an animal, the body being the eleventh;
eight are the corners, two the joints, the body being the eleventh;
he cuts it commensurate with cattle.
It should be cut commensurate with the stomas;
[namely] of nine cubits;
it is commensurate with the ninefold [stoma] through splendor.
It should be cut of fifteen cubits;
it is commensurate with the fifteenfold [stoma];
the fifteenfold [stoma] is a thunderbolt;
it is commensurate with the thunderbolt.
It should be cut of seventeen cubits;
it is commensurate with the seventeenfold [stoma];
Prajāpati is seventeenfold;
it is commensurate with Prajāpati.
It should be cut of twenty-one cubits;
it is commensurate with the twenty-onefold [stoma];
yonder sun is twenty-onefold;
it is commensurate with yonder sun.
It should be cut of twenty-seven cubits;
it is commensurate with the twenty-sevenfold [stoma];
these worlds are thrice-nine;
it is commensurate with these worlds.
It should be cut of thirty-three cubits;
it is commensurate with the thirty-threefold [stoma];
thirty-three are the deities;
it is commensurate with the deities.
It should be cut commensurate with the meters.
Of five cubits, it is commensurate with the paṅkti meter.
Of six cubits, it is commensurate with the aticchandas.
Of seven cubits, it is commensurate with the śakvarī.
Of eight cubits, it is commensurate with the gāyatrī.
Of nine cubits, it is commensurate with the bṛhatī.
Of ten cubits, it is commensurate with the virāj.
Of eleven cubits, it is commensurate with the triṣṭubh.
Of twelve cubits, it is commensurate with the jagatī.
Of thirteen cubits, it is commensurate with the Sādhya gods.
Large, unmeasured should it be cut.
From the sacrificial post indeed the gods went to the heavenly world;
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Whatever [tree] is taller than a man, that he should cut;
this indeed is the measure of the sacrificial post;
as large as the sacrificial post is, that he cuts.
There is a top-ring.
From the top-ring indeed the gods went to the heavenly world;
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Indra indeed hurled the thunderbolt at Vṛtra.
As much of it as was grasped, that was shattered.
They made that into the top-ring.
In that there is a top-ring,
[it is] for firmness, for non-slackness of the grasp.
It is so much [in size];
for so much of it was grasped.
He should not let it project beyond the sacrificial post.
If he were to let it project, women would have swellings.
He should let it project as much as the topmost joint of a finger;
[it is then] measured by a joint of the sacrifice,
for the non-repulsion of cattle.
What is redundant in the sacrifice becomes redundant for the Sādhya gods;
what projects from the sacrificial post is redundant in the sacrifice;
thereby he gratifies the Sādhya gods.
He should make the top-ring of another tree than that of which the sacrificial post is, for him of whom he should desire: 'May I produce a rival for him.'
Other trees indeed are the rivals of this one;
he causes a rival to mount upon his world;
he deprives him of a world.

26.5.m:
"For the earth thee! For the atmosphere thee! For the sky thee!"—with this he sprinkles the sacrificial post.
He sprinkles it for these worlds.
For this world the audumbarī [post] is measured out, for that [world] the sacrificial post;
therefore he sprinkles the sacrificial post pointing away from here, and the audumbarī [pointing] hither from there,
for the keeping apart of these two worlds.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, [thee]…"—with this he takes the spade.
Impelled by Savitṛ, he takes it with the deities.
"Here do I cut off the neck of the Rakṣas"—[with this he digs];
the rival is the Rakṣas;
he cuts off the neck of his rival.
"May the worlds, the seats of the fathers, be cleansed!"—[with this he sprinkles the pit];
he cleanses it;
moreover, what cruel [wound] they inflict on it in digging, that he appeases.
He pours in [water] containing barley.
Barley is food; the sacrificial post is splendor;
he places splendor in food.
Barley is food; the sacrificial post is a thunderbolt;
he places the thunderbolt in food.
"I spread thee, a world, the seat of the fathers"—[with this he spreads the barhis in the pit];
if they were to set it up without barhis, it would be a pit-post;
the plants are on the northern half of this [earth];
he sets it up on the northern half of this [earth];
he has made it not a pit-post.
"Thou art of easy approach, going in front of the leaders"—with this he throws down a chip of the sacrificial post.
Rough indeed is it when hewn, unfit for cattle;
he sets it up with its skin.
"May heaven and earth be filled with ghee!"—[with this he besprinkles it];
with ghee he moistens heaven and earth;
therefore plants, [even when] unanointed, shine.
"May god Savitṛ anoint thee with honey!"—[with this he anoints it];
ghee is the honey of the gods;
he makes it fit for offering, sacrificial.
"Make the plants possess good fruit!"—with this he fixes the head-ring;
he causes the plants to bear fruit;
therefore they ripen without being ploughed.
He anoints the agniṣṭhā with ghee;
ghee is splendor, the sacrificer is the agniṣṭhā;
with splendor he makes the sacrificer prosper.
He anoints continuously to the end,
for the continuity of splendor,
for non-interruption.
Against whomever he bears hatred, for him he should interrupt [the anointing];
he deprives him of splendor.
He anoints on all sides;
from all sides he makes him prosper with splendor.
"Thou hast touched the sky with thy tip; thou hast filled the atmosphere with thy middle; thou hast made firm the earth with thy butt"—
the sacrificial post is a thunderbolt;
[this is] for the keeping apart of these worlds.
"Those dwellings of you two…"—with this [verse] addressed to Viṣṇu he brings it down;
the sacrificial post belongs to Viṣṇu;
with its own deity he brings it down.
"Behold the deeds of Viṣṇu…"—with this [verse] addressed to Viṣṇu he arranges it;
the sacrificial post belongs to Viṣṇu;
with its own deity he arranges it.
He should make the agniṣṭhā [face] toward the fire;
fire is splendor, the sacrificer is the agniṣṭhā;
with splendor he makes the sacrificer prosper.
Against whomever he bears hatred, for him he should bend it this way or that way;
he deprives him of splendor.
"Thee, winning the Brahman, winning the Kṣatra…"—
he expresses a blessing;
"Strengthen the Brahman! Strengthen the Kṣatra!"—
he strengthens for him the blessing that was expressed.
"That highest step of Viṣṇu…"—with this [verse] addressed to Viṣṇu he looks up at it;
the sacrificial post belongs to Viṣṇu;
with its own deity he looks up at it.

26.6.m:
He should make half of the post-hole on the altar, half outside the altar,
for the obtaining of the measured and the unmeasured.
He should not make the butt visible;
if he were to make the butt visible, it would be a pit-post.
He should not let it go untied around [with the cord];
if he were to let it go untied around, the sacrificer would become hungry, the adhvaryu would become naked.
The cord is vigor;
he ties it around against the middle;
he places vigor in the middle, both in the sacrificer and in offspring;
therefore vigor invigorates offspring from the middle.
He should set it up without a cord for him whom he hates;
the cord is vigor;
he deprives him of vigor.
He should push it upward or downward for him whom he hates;
the cord is vigor;
he deprives him of vigor.
He should tie it around high up, far above, if he should desire, "May it rain!";
it rains from the waters, from the plants, when it rains;
the cord is the plants;
he brings the plants closer to the rain;
it rains quickly.
He should tie it around low down, far below, if he should desire, "May it not rain!";
it rains from the waters, from the plants, when it rains;
the cord is the plants;
he puts the plants further from the rain;
it takes a long time to rain.
"Thou art girded around; may the divine clans gird thee around!"—[with this he ties it];
these divine clans are the plants;
the cord is the plants;
the sacrificial post is the sacrificer;
he turns offspring face to face toward the sacrificer.
"Around this man [pour] wealth!"—
wealth is cattle;
he makes him prosper with cattle.
"Glide over the back of the atmosphere!"—with this he inserts the svaru-chip;
for it has the atmosphere as its deity at this time, being above this world, as it were, and below that one;
with its own deity he inserts it.
The butt belongs to the fathers;
what is above the butt belongs to men;
the region of the cord belongs to the plants;
what is above the cord belongs to the All-gods;
the head-ring belongs to Indra;
what is leftover belongs to the Sādhyas;
in this very manner are the fathers, men, and gods in these worlds;
the sacrificial post belongs to all deities;
with all deities he makes it prosper.
He should make [the post] bent inward from the side of the fire, bent outward on the outer side;
by what is bent inward from the side of the fire he makes room for the sacrificer; by what is bent outward on the outer side, he thrusts away the rival from the sacrifice.
By means of the sacrifice the gods went to the heavenly world;
they desired, "May no other know this world of ours!";
they obstructed the quarters;
because they obstructed (ayopayan) the quarters, that is why the post is called post (yūpa);
having gone to that world, they thirsted;
they knew, "From offerings given from there we have come here";
they gave this [post] back;
thereby they prospered.
This indeed is the warp of the sacrifice;
this is the highest footstep;
just as a web is stretched from a peg, so from here the sacrifice is stretched.
Formerly, pulling it down, they used to offer it [the post itself];
in that he offers the svaru-chip,
[it is] for the ransom of this very [post];
ransomed for him, pleased, rendered auspicious, it attends upon these worlds.
"May thy smoke go to the sky, thy flame to the atmosphere, thy ash to the earth, svāhā!"—
he makes it go to these very worlds.

26.8.m:
He recites [a verse] addressed to Savitṛ;
impelled by Savitṛ, he begets it.
He recites [a verse] having one deity;
in a single way he begets it.
If he were to recite [a verse] having two deities, he would generate for him a rival as a second.
He recites [a verse] addressed to Heaven and Earth,
for the comprehension of born offspring.
He should not recite [a verse] containing [the word] "smoke";
for when Indra slew Vṛtra, wherever he had failed, smoke arose from there;
he who is born from failure—
he becomes destructive to his failure.
If [the fire] being churned should not be born, he should recite [a verse] slaying the Rakṣases,
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
He should recite without breathing out;
for the Rakṣases seek to destroy the sacrifice along the breath;
he makes an obstacle for the Rakṣases.
He should recite a gāyatrī for a brāhmaṇa;
for the brāhmaṇa belongs to the gāyatrī;
a triṣṭubh for a rājanya;
for the rājanya belongs to the triṣṭubh;
a jagatī for a vaiśya;
for the vaiśya belongs to the jagatī;
according to the respective meters.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ [thee]…"—with this he takes the cord;
impelled by Savitṛ, he takes it with the deities.
"With the noose of the cosmic order, O divine offering, I fasten thee"—
that which is sacrificial [and] fit for sacrifice is the noose of cosmic order;
with that he seizes it.
"Welcome to such-and-such [deity]"—
to whichever deity he seizes [it], for that very [deity] he makes it welcome.
"Overpower the human!"—
the sacrificial post is the thunderbolt, cattle are human;
with the thunderbolt he restrains cattle for him.
Bare and hewn, this [post] is unfit for sacrifice;
when he binds the animal [to it],
he makes it fit for sacrifice, sacrificial.
From the front, cattle approach the sacrificial essence;
deities in this [sacrifice] are the vital breaths;
when they propitiate [it] pointing west from the front,
they propitiate half of the deities.
"From the waters, from the plants, [thee]…"—
from the waters, from the plants, is this animal seized;
from the waters and from the plants he sprinkles it.
"May thy mother consent, may thy father [consent]…"—
for his father and mother are the consenters.
The waters are all deities;
the animal belongs to all deities;
with all the deities he enriches it.
To much that is unfit for sacrifice, unsuited for sacrifice, does this animal go;
the waters are fit for sacrifice, sacrificial;
when he sprinkles with water,
he makes it fit for sacrifice, sacrificial.
"Thou art the drink of the waters"—with this he takes up water;
those portions that are within, those he makes fit for sacrifice.
"Pleasing offering to the gods, full of ghee"—with this he sprinkles down upon the chest;
those portions that are underneath, those he makes fit for sacrifice.
He sprinkles on all sides;
on all sides he enriches it with sacrificial essence.
The head of the sacrifice is the butter-libation; the animal is the self;
when, having poured the butter-libation, he anoints the animal,
he places the head of the sacrifice onto the self.
"May Vāyu unite with thy breath"—
he makes Vāyu unite with his breath.
"Thy limbs with those worthy of sacrifice"—
those worthy of sacrifice are the deities;
he makes his limbs unite with the deities.
"The lord of the sacrifice with the benediction"—
with the benediction he enriches the sacrificer.

26.9.m:
He chooses the priests;
the meters are the priests;
he chooses the meters.
When chosen [and] joined by him, the vaṣaṭ-calls are harnessed before them.
He chooses the hotṛ;
thereby he chooses the jagatī among the meters.
He chooses the āgnīdhra;
thereby he chooses the paṅkti among the meters.
He chooses the two adhvaryus;
thereby he chooses the aticchandas among the meters.
Between the hotṛ and the two adhvaryus he chooses the āgnīdhra;
therefore Agni has entered into the midst of the plants.
Since he sacrifices last,
therefore, being churned from above, he is born.
He chooses the maitrāvaruṇa;
thereby he chooses the gāyatrī among the meters.
He chooses the brāhmaṇācchaṃsin;
thereby he chooses the triṣṭubh among the meters.
He chooses the potṛ;
thereby he chooses the uṣṇih among the meters.
He chooses the neṣṭṛ;
thereby he chooses the kakubh among the meters.
Born behind, as it were, is this meter, the anuṣṭubh;
born behind is this office of the hotṛ, namely that of the acchāvāka;
therefore they only summon the acchāvāka, they do not choose him.
Now these Āprī [verses]:
Prajāpati, having created offspring, thought himself emptied out;
he saw these Āprī [verses];
with them he propitiated himself.
The sacrifice is Prajāpati; the sacrificer is the self of the sacrifice.
Since these are performed in the first half of the sacrifice,
he propitiates the sacrificer right from the beginning.
He sacrifices the fore-offerings;
the fore-offerings are the vital breaths;
he fore-sacrifices the vital breaths;
that is the fore-offeringness of the fore-offerings.
He should propitiate without breathing out for him whom he wishes: "May he go to full life";
the fore-offerings are the vital breaths;
he establishes unbroken vital breaths in him;
he goes to full life.
The omentum of the animal is the self; the Āprī [verses] of the sacrificer are the self;
since the last Āprī verse encompasses the omentum,
the self encompasses the self,
for the continuity, for the non-interruption of the sacrifice.
There are eleven fore-offerings, eleven after-offerings, eleven by-offerings;
so many are the deities;
the animal belongs to all deities;
with all the deities he enriches it.
Thirty-three are the deities that drink Soma, thirty-three that do not drink Soma;
the eight Vasus, the eleven Rudras, the twelve Ādityas, the vaṣaṭ-call, and Prajāpati—these are the Soma-drinkers;
the fore-offerings, the after-offerings, [and] the by-offerings—these are the non-Soma-drinkers;
therefore they are performed outside the sacrifice.
With Soma he pleases the gods who drink Soma;
with the animal, those who do not drink Soma;
both of these become pleased with him at the sacrifice.
This indeed Śyāparṇa Sāyakāyana said to Aṣāḍha Kaiśina when he led him away from the Soma:
"If thou shalt lead me away from the Soma, thou shalt lead me away together with both gods, both the Soma-drinkers and the non-Soma-drinkers;
deprived of gods thy sacrifice will be abandoned;
and in whatever direction they shall lead me, from there [enemies] attacking will conquer thee."
Having deliberated: "From here there is no fear for us," they led [him] toward the south and west;
thence the Kuntis, attacking, conquered the Pañcālas.
He who leads one who knows thus away from the Soma, leads him away together with both gods, the Soma-drinkers and the non-Soma-drinkers;
deprived of gods his sacrifice is abandoned;
and in whatever direction they lead him, from there [enemies] attacking conquer him.

26.10.m:
The gods and the Asuras did equally at the sacrifice;
whatever the gods did, that the Asuras did.
Then the gods saw this Upāṃśu [cup];
they saw the sacrifice as concluding in this [cup];
in it alone they brought the sacrifice to conclusion.
Eight times they pressed;
thereby they obtained the gāyatrī meter.
Eleven times [they pressed];
thereby [they obtained] the triṣṭubh.
Twelve times [they pressed];
thereby [they obtained] the jagatī.
Three times they drew [it];
thereby they obtained the Soma-pressings.
Those gods, having enclosed as much as the sacrifice was, stepped up to offer.
Those Asuras saw: "The gods are offering."
Having raised the vajra, they rushed at them.
The gods shut them off from these worlds with the Antaryāma [cup].
Then the gods flourished, the Asuras perished.
He for whom they offer the Upāṃśu knowing thus, and who knowing thus offers, shuts off his rival from these worlds; he himself flourishes, his rival perishes.
Now indeed Āruṇa Aupaveśi used to say:
"Why should he sacrifice who does not know that the sacrifice is to be concluded in the Upāṃśu?"
Eight times he presses;
thereby he obtains the gāyatrī meter.
Eleven times [he presses];
thereby [he obtains] the triṣṭubh.
Twelve times [he presses];
thereby [he obtains] the jagatī.
Three times he draws [it];
thereby he obtains the Soma-pressings.
Having concluded the sacrifice in the Upāṃśu, he proceeds with that which is concluded, unharmed, uninjured.
He should press eight [and] eight [times] for one desiring spiritual luster;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
he disposes the gāyatrī at the opening of the sacrifice;
in the gāyatrī, in fiery energy, in his own womb, in the heavenly world, the opening of the sacrifice is extended.
He who knows thus becomes eminent.
He should not say: "Thou art taken with a support (upayāma)."
The earth indeed is the support;
by it he would block the breaths.
They do not stretch out a strainer;
the atmosphere indeed is the strainer;
if he were to enclose the breaths with the atmosphere, he would perish.
This [wind] which purifies, he indeed is breath;
inhaling this [breath], we breathe.
Since he does not say: "Thou art taken with a support", nor do they stretch out a strainer,
therefore this [wind], unenclosed, blows at will this way and that way;
therefore we breathe at will.
The Upāṃśu is breath-purifying;
with the breaths alone he purifies it.
He takes six shoots;
therefore there are six breaths; he purifies them in pairs;
therefore the breaths are in pairs.
He holds down the Upāṃśu [shoot] with the Upāṃśusavana stone;
the Upāṃśusavana stone is breath;
the Upāṃśu is breath;
from breath alone he holds down breath.
The Upāṃśusavana stone is above, the shoots below;
the Upāṃśusavana stone is breath, the shoots are offspring;
in offspring alone he places breath;
therefore breath is supreme.
